# Helping build Hornhoof

Thank you for wanting to help. Hornhoof is young, and every report, idea and change shapes it. This page says how work gets into Hornhoof, so your time is not lost.

## Ways to help, with or without code

- Report a problem: open an issue with "Bug report". Say what you did, what you expected and what happened. Logs help, but remove keys and passwords from them first.
- Suggest an idea: open an issue with "Idea". Start with the problem you have, not the solution. "I lose track of which agent waits for me" tells us more than "add a button".
- Translate. English is the main language of Hornhoof, and every other language is a translation of it. The screens have Russian now. Better words, or a whole new language, are welcome; see "Adding a language" below.
- Write templates. Departments, roles and skills live in `templates/`. They must fit any company: no company, product, industry or programming language of one particular user in them.
- Fix and build. Issues marked `good first issue` are small and well described, and `help wanted` marks bigger ones we would love help with.

Security problems never go into a public issue. [SECURITY.md](SECURITY.md) says how to report them privately.

## How a change gets in

Hornhoof works like most large open projects: you propose a change as a pull request, the checks run, a maintainer reviews it, and a maintainer merges it.

1. Agree first on anything big. For a new feature, a new screen or a change to how agents work, open an issue or comment on one before you write code. A short "I want to do X like this" saves a pull request that cannot be taken. Typos, small fixes and translations need no issue.
2. Fork the repository and make a branch from `main`. Name it after what it does, like `fix-invite-link` or `machines-tab-phone`.
3. Write the test first. Write a test that shows what should happen, run it and watch it fail, then write the code that makes it pass. A change without a test that failed first is not done.
4. Run the checks on your machine, as "Checks before you push" says. They must be green.
5. Commit with a sign-off, as "Sign your work" says.
6. Open a pull request against `main` and fill in the template. Keep it to one topic: two small pull requests get in faster than one big one.
7. The checks run on GitHub. Tests, type checks and the sign-off check must pass before review.
8. A maintainer reviews it. Answer review comments with new commits on the same branch, and do not force-push over a branch under review. When a comment is unclear, ask.
9. A maintainer merges it as one commit with the pull request's title. Your sign-off goes with it.

We try to give every pull request a first answer within a week. If yours waits longer, a friendly comment on it is welcome.

## Set up

You need Node 22, Docker, and bash and jq for the tests of the installer and the updater (`brew install jq` on a Mac, `apt install jq` on Debian or Ubuntu).

```sh
git clone https://github.com/<you>/hornhoof.git
cd hornhoof
npm install
npm test
```

To run it, start Postgres, then the server with `DATABASE_URL` and `HORNHOOF_SECRET` set (`npm run dev`) and the web app with `npm run dev -w apps/web`. The web app sends `/api` to port 8080. To run everything in Docker instead, see [Run it from source with Docker](guide/install.md#run-it-from-source-with-docker) in the guide.

The repository has four parts:

- `apps/server`: the server, its database and the tools agents call.
- `apps/web`: the screens people use.
- `apps/node`: the node that runs agents on a machine.
- `packages/protocol`: what the server and the nodes say to each other.

## Checks before you push

```sh
npm test
npx tsc -p apps/server/tsconfig.json --noEmit
npx tsc -p apps/web/tsconfig.json --noEmit
npx tsc -p apps/node/tsconfig.json --noEmit
```

Run `tsc` for each part you changed. After a change in `packages/protocol`, rebuild it with `npx tsc -p packages/protocol/tsconfig.json` before you test the server or the node. After a change in `scripts/`, run `shellcheck` over the script.

A change to the database schema gets a new migration from `npx drizzle-kit generate` in `apps/server`. Never edit a migration that is already on `main`.

## Rules for the code and the words

These are the same rules everyone follows, people and agents alike. [AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md) has them in short.

- Code, comments, commit messages and the README are in English.
- Every screen ships in English and Russian. A new string goes into both `apps/web/src/i18n/en.ts` and `apps/web/src/i18n/ru.ts`, in plain words a person outside IT reads without effort.
- No em dashes anywhere, in code, texts or translations. In Russian write "е" instead of "ё".
- Match the code around you: its naming, how much it comments and how it does things.
- Do not reformat files you did not change, and do not run a formatter over the whole repository.
- A screen you change must work at phone width, with no sideways scroll. Add screenshots in English and Russian to the pull request.
- Templates in `templates/` stay general, and `templates/SOURCES.md` names where each text comes from and under which licence. Do not copy text from a source whose licence does not allow it.

## Adding a language

All text people see lives in `apps/web/src/i18n/`: `en.ts` is the source, and `ru.ts` is its Russian translation. A new language is a new file next to them with every key of `en.ts`, plus its name in `LANGUAGES` in `index.tsx`. Write it in plain words, the way a person outside IT speaks, and check the screens at phone width, where longer words wrap first. Open an issue before you start, so two people do not translate the same language.

## Commit messages

One line in English that says what changes for the person using Hornhoof, as a plain sentence:

```
Show the machine an employee runs on
Keep a machine command in a conversation with its code hidden
```

Add a short body when the why is not obvious. The pull request's title follows the same rule, since it becomes the commit on `main`.

## Sign your work

Like the Linux kernel, Hornhoof asks every commit in a pull request to carry a `Signed-off-by` line. With it you state that you wrote the change, or have the right to send it, under the [Developer Certificate of Origin](https://developercertificate.org/). Git adds the line for you:

```sh
git commit -s -m "Show the machine an employee runs on"
```

It uses the name and email in your git settings. Forgot it? `git commit --amend -s` fixes the last commit, and `git rebase --signoff main` fixes all of them on your branch, before review starts.

## Your contribution's licence

Hornhoof is under the [MIT License](LICENSE). By sending a pull request you agree that your change is published under it too.

## Working with AI agents

Using an AI agent to write your change is fine; Hornhoof itself is built with many. Point your agent to [AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md). You still answer for the pull request: read every line, run it, and make sure the tests prove what it claims. Leave the agent's "Generated with" and co-author lines out of commits.

## Be kind

Everyone here follows the [code of conduct](CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md).
# Security

Hornhoof is built so that your company stays yours. It runs on your own machines, its agents work in locked boxes there, and only people your company invites can sign in. This page says exactly how, and how to tell us if you find a hole.

## Your company lives on your machines

- There is no Hornhoof cloud and no account with us. The server, its database and its files run in Docker on your own computers.
- The company's database sits on up to three of your machines: one leads, and the others keep full copies. Copies go only to machines of the CEO and of people who see the whole company.
- No single machine is the company. If the leading machine stops, an up-to-date copy takes over within about a minute, under the same link.
- Every 6 hours and before each update, the leading machine makes a backup, signs it and encrypts it. Up to three other company machines keep the latest two and cannot open them. Only the machines that may hold the data, and the recovery file you save in Settings, can. A machine's owner can untick "Keep company backups".
- A machine that only does work keeps no copy of the database, only those sealed backups when its owner allows them.
- Your machines talk to each other over encrypted connections, and each one proves who it is with a certificate from the company's own authority. The port they talk on takes only machines with such a certificate.
- Hornhoof looks for new builds at GitHub's container registry, anonymously, as `docker pull` does. An update runs exactly the images it checked, by their digests. It saves the database first, and goes back to the old build by itself if the new one does not answer.
- The web app loads nothing from other sites. Its scripts and fonts come from your own server.

## Agents work in locked boxes

- On each machine, every plan runs in a Docker container of its own. The company's API keys run in the server's built-in node, which is a container too.
- No folder of your computer is put into these containers, and none of them gets the Docker socket. An agent sees its container, not your computer.
- The containers drop every Linux capability but the few needed to run agents as another user, and nothing inside them can gain new privileges.
- Inside its container, an agent runs as a user of its own. It cannot read the node's key or change the programs the node starts.
- The built-in node is not on the database's network, so no agent there can reach the database.
- Tests and other work on lent machines run in a fresh folder, as a user that reaches neither the node's key nor the agents' folders. The folder is removed afterwards.

## Sign-ins and keys

- You sign in to each plan yourself, on your own machine. The sign-in is sealed to your machines: Hornhoof stores it so that the server and the copies of the company cannot open it, and only your own machines can. An Antigravity sign-in stays on the machine it was made on. A personal plan's work goes only to its owner's machines.
- Before anything leaves a machine, the node puts `[hidden]` in place of its own key, the plans' tokens, API keys and repository tokens, in terminals and in every output.
- API keys, repository tokens and the link's key are stored encrypted with AES-256-GCM, under the secret in your `.env`. Passwords are stored as scrypt hashes. Sessions, invite links, install commands and the keys nodes connect with are kept only as SHA-256 hashes.
- The command that connects a machine works once, for 24 hours. When the orchestrator makes one, its code shows only on the CEO's screen and never reaches an agent.
- "Disconnect" on a machine's card takes its keys away at once, so a lost or stolen machine can no longer reach the company.

## People see only what they are given

- Until a company is set up, only the setup link from the server's own log can set it up.
- Failed sign-ins are capped: 10 in 15 minutes for one email from one address, and 50 in an hour for one email.
- Session cookies cannot be read by scripts on the page, and travel only over https when Hornhoof is on https.
- A change, or a terminal connection, that comes from another site's page is refused, even with your cookie.
- Each person sees only the departments they are given.
- Only the owner of a plan types into its agents' terminals. Everyone else who reaches the employee watches.
- The orchestrator takes orders about people, access, plans, machines, repositories, the public link and updates only in a turn that answers the CEO.
- The orchestrator's database role cannot read or write passwords, tokens or keys, nor the tables of sign-ins, invites and install commands.
- Every machine lends its power to the company's pool by default, and agents still work only inside their boxes there. Root on a machine goes to the orchestrator only when that machine's owner opens access to it, for one day at most, and only in turns that answer the CEO. Nobody else can open it, the CEO included.

## The hornhoof.org link

The link, like `https://acme.hornhoof.org`, lets people open your company from anywhere without opening anything on your router.

- It is optional. Untick "Open Hornhoof on the internet" at setup, or remove the link in Settings, and people open Hornhoof only from your own network.
- Your server opens the tunnel itself, over an encrypted connection to hornhoof.org whose certificate it checks.
- The tunnel carries traffic only to the Hornhoof app, never to anything else on your network, whatever hornhoof.org asks for.
- hornhoof.org has no accounts and holds none of your company's data. For each link it keeps only the name, a hash of its key, when and from which address the link was made, and when its tunnel was last seen. For a company on several machines it also keeps which machine holds the link, and one flag that says whether the company's copy is up to date.
- The browser's https connection ends at hornhoof.org. It opens each request to see which company it is for, passes it on through that company's tunnel, and keeps no copy. If you want no one in between, use your own domain with your own TLS, as [the install guide](guide/install.md#how-do-people-open-it-from-other-computers) shows.
- Traffic between your machines that goes through hornhoof.org is encrypted from one machine to the other, so hornhoof.org sees only encrypted bytes.
- hornhoof.org decides nothing for your company. A link points to one machine at a time, and that tells two machines which of them leads. A company with no public link keeps a hidden name there for that alone. Three or more machines that are always on choose the leader by vote among themselves.

## What you decide

- Who sees the whole company. Those people see everything, and so do the orchestrator and anyone with access to Operations, whose head reads the database.
- Which plan a department uses. Agents on one plan share its sign-in and can read each other's folders, so work that must stay apart gets a plan of its own.
- What a repository token may do. It goes to the machines that run the department's agents and tests, so a fine-grained token for one repository is best.
- Which AI makers you use. Agents send their prompts and code to those makers, as the programs do outside Hornhoof.

Keep `.env` and the recovery file like passwords: the secret in `.env` opens the stored keys, and the recovery file opens the backups.

## Report a vulnerability

Report it privately on GitHub, not in a public issue: open the repository's **Security** tab and click **Report a vulnerability**. Only the maintainers see the report.

Tell us:

- what an attacker can do, and what they need first (an account, a connected machine, a link);
- the steps to show it, as short as you can make them;
- the version: the build under Versions in the account menu, or the commit you run;
- whether it touches the server, the web app, a node, the installer or the relay at hornhoof.org.

We aim to answer within a week, agree on a fix and a date with you, and name you in the fix unless you ask us not to.

You are welcome to test all of this as hard as you like on your own install.

## Which versions

Only the latest build on `main` gets security fixes. Update from the banner in Hornhoof, or run the install line again.
# Code of conduct

Hornhoof is built by people from different countries, with different experience and different first languages. We want everyone here to feel welcome, whether they report their first bug or send their hundredth pull request.

## What we ask

- Be kind and patient. Assume good intent, and explain instead of judging.
- Talk about the work, not the person. Criticize code and ideas, never people.
- Plain words over clever ones: many of us read in a second language.
- Welcome newcomers and help them find their way.
- Accept a "no" on a change gracefully, and say "no" with a reason.

## What is not acceptable

- Insults, harassment, threats, or jokes about anyone's identity, body or background.
- Sexual language or images.
- Publishing someone's private information without their permission.
- Pushing on after someone asked you to stop.

## Our full rules

We follow the [Contributor Covenant, version 2.1](https://www.contributor-covenant.org/version/2/1/code_of_conduct/), in issues, pull requests, discussions and every other place where Hornhoof's work happens.

## Reporting

If something here made you feel unwelcome or unsafe, tell the maintainers privately: open the repository's **Security** tab, click **Report a vulnerability** and say it is about conduct. Only the maintainers see it. We look into every report, keep the reporter's name to ourselves, and may warn, block or remove anyone who breaks these rules.
import { writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';
import { tmpdir } from 'node:os';
import { join } from 'node:path';
import { isLinkName } from '@hornhoof/protocol/link';
import { z } from 'zod';
import type { Client, TunnelSettings } from './tunnel.js';

/* The parts of the relay's tunnel Hornhoof takes: the frps server and how to reach it. */
const Server = z.object({
  serverAddr: z.string().regex(/^[A-Za-z0-9.-]{1,253}$/),
  serverPort: z.number().int().min(1).max(65535),
  protocol: z.literal('wss'),
});

/* A TOML string: JSON escapes quotes, backslashes and line breaks the way TOML reads them. */
const quoted = (text: string) => JSON.stringify(text);

/* The frpc config for the link. It is written here and not taken as the relay sends it, so the tunnel carries the
   link's traffic to the app and to nothing else on the company's network, whatever the relay says. */
function configFor(settings: TunnelSettings): string {
  const server = Server.parse(settings.tunnel);
  if (!isLinkName(settings.name)) throw new Error(`${settings.name} is not a link name`);
  if (!settings.key) throw new Error('the link has no key');
  return `serverAddr = ${quoted(server.serverAddr)}
serverPort = ${server.serverPort}
loginFailExit = false
log.to = "console"
log.level = "info"
log.disablePrintColor = true
transport.protocol = "wss"
transport.tls.trustedCaFile = "/etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt"
transport.heartbeatInterval = 30
transport.heartbeatTimeout = 90
metadatas.name = ${quoted(settings.name)}
metadatas.key = ${quoted(settings.key)}

[[proxies]]
name = ${quoted(settings.name)}
type = "http"
subdomain = ${quoted(settings.name)}
localIP = "app"
localPort = 8080
requestHeaders.set.x-forwarded-proto = "https"
`;
}

/* frpc with the link's config, written where only this container's user reads it. */
export function clientFor(settings: TunnelSettings, dir = tmpdir()): Client {
  const path = join(dir, 'frpc.toml');
  writeFileSync(path, configFor(settings), { mode: 0o600 });
  return { program: 'frpc', args: ['-c', path] };
}
